# What the stack needs that nothing else in this repository does. The single source of all
# four, loaded by direnv through `dotenv_if_exists stack/.env` in .envrc - the same
# mechanism backend/.env goes through - and read a second time by compose itself, which
# auto-loads the .env beside the compose file it is given. Both routes read this file, so
# the two agree by construction.
#
# Three of the four are names compose.yaml dereferences. COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME is not: it
# is one of compose's OWN variables, read by the CLI and never written into the file, so
# the no-defaults invariant in CLAUDE.md does not reach it.
#
# Committed, and holding development values only. Nothing here is a credential: the stack
# publishes no secret and the database on its network takes trust auth.
#
# The two host directories the loaders mount are NOT here. They are facts about the
# backend's data, so backend/.env names them, and a variable is named in one file only.

# What `docker compose -f stack/compose.yaml up` runs: the three images `poe image` and
# `pnpm run image` build locally, under the tag those bake files give them. A release
# overrides both with <namespace>/<repository> and the release tag, the shell environment
# outranking this file, so its values win without editing it.
IMAGE_PREFIX=expense-tracker
IMAGE_TAG=dev

# What a checkout's stack is CALLED, overriding compose.yaml's `name:`. Without it a local
# `up` and an `oci://` release both answer to expense-tracker-stack: one project, one
# `expense-tracker-stack_database-data`, each adopting the other's database. A release
# host sets its own, or takes the file's name. `-p` outranks this; nothing else does.
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=expense-tracker-dev

# The origin the BROWSER calls, not a name on the compose network. It must be the host
# port the backend service publishes, so moving the API off 8000 means editing both.
API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
